Got it — I checked the actual file at:
C:\Users\gagan\Desktop\benchmark_hermes+fabric\prereqs\fixtures\task01_explain_jwt\auth\jwt.py

Here’s exactly how this implementation works.

What it issues

- Algorithm: HS256 (symmetric HMAC)
- Secret: hardcoded string dev-secret-change-me
- Access TTL: 900s (15 min)
- Refresh TTL: 7 days

issue(sub)
- Creates two JWTs with same sub and iat:
  1) access token claims: {sub, iat, exp}
  2) refresh token claims: {sub, iat, exp, typ: "refresh"}
- Returns {"access": ..., "refresh": ...}

Validation flow (validate)

validate(token):
1) Decodes with pyjwt.decode(token, SECRET, algorithms=[ALGO])
   - This verifies signature and exp by default.
2) If ExpiredSignatureError or InvalidTokenError -> returns None
3) Additional type gate:
   - if claims["typ"] == "refresh" -> reject (None)
4) Otherwise returns claims (valid access token)

So access token acceptance criteria are:
- valid HS256 signature with SECRET
- not expired
- not explicitly tagged as typ=refresh

Refresh flow (refresh)

refresh(refresh_token):
1) Decodes with same SECRET/ALGO
   - catches InvalidTokenError (includes expired/invalid signature/etc)
2) Requires claims["typ"] == "refresh"
   - otherwise reject
3) Calls issue(claims["sub"]) to mint a brand-new access+refresh pair

So this does refresh token rotation in practice (new refresh issued each time), but without server-side invalidation of old refresh tokens.

What is NOT checked

- No issuer (iss) validation
- No audience (aud) validation
- No jti / nonce / token ID tracking
- No revocation list / denylist
- No session store / refresh family tracking
- No user-status check (disabled user, password change, etc.)
- No required-claims enforcement beyond what code reads
- No key rotation / kid handling (single static secret)

Security implications / edge cases

1) Hardcoded weak/dev secret
- Biggest risk. If leaked/guessable, attacker can forge both access and refresh tokens.

2) Refresh replay risk
- Stolen refresh token can be reused until expiry (7 days), because old refresh tokens are not revoked after use.

3) No context binding
- Tokens are bearer-only; not bound to device/IP/session.

4) Minimal claim model
- No scopes/roles/permissions in token; authorization must happen elsewhere.

5) Type confusion mostly handled
- Access endpoint blocks typ=refresh.
- Refresh endpoint requires typ=refresh.
- Good basic separation for this fixture.

Bottom line

- This is a clean minimal benchmark implementation.
- It correctly verifies signature+expiry and separates access vs refresh by typ.
- For production, the main upgrades are:
  1) strong externalized secret/key management (+ rotation),
  2) refresh-token revocation/rotation tracking,
  3) issuer/audience checks,
  4) session/user-state validation at refresh time.
